  "summary": "A technique where paint is applied thickly to create texture and dimensionality, with visible brushstrokes or palette knife marks.",
  "description": "Impasto creates a three-dimensional quality on the canvas, with paint built up in layers that cast shadows and catch light differently. This technique emerged from Renaissance practices and was notably developed by artists like Rembrandt and Van Gogh to add expressive physicality to their work.",
  "culturalContext": "Impasto — paint applied in thick, knife- and brush-loaded relief — turned surface into subject: Rembrandt's late-peak gold jewelry ridges, Van Gogh's line-brushed sky furrows, de Kooning's anatomical slabs, Anselm Kiefer's straw-laden crusts. The technique's shadow-casting physicality reads as evidence of hand and decision; acrylic and gel media industrialized it, and \"impasto relief\" is now a scanning-culture category (macro photography, raking light cataloguing) of its own.",
